WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:01.999
Instructor: All right, so the first of many

00:01.999 --> 00:04.246
different commands we're going to see in this section

00:04.246 --> 00:08.370
is actually one we've seen before, git checkout.

00:08.370 --> 00:10.748
So when we learned how to first switch branches,

00:10.748 --> 00:12.891
I used git switch, that's what I showed you.

00:12.891 --> 00:15.240
It's the newer way of doing things.

00:15.240 --> 00:17.371
But the longstanding way of switching branches

00:17.371 --> 00:19.488
is using git checkout, but also,

00:19.488 --> 00:22.650
git checkout does a lot of other things.

00:22.650 --> 00:23.745
So in this video, I'm gonna show you one

00:23.745 --> 00:25.998
of those other things.

00:25.998 --> 00:27.980
As a reminder, a lot of people think

00:27.980 --> 00:30.300
the git checkout command does too much

00:30.300 --> 00:32.403
which is why new commands were added

00:32.403 --> 00:34.486
including git switch and git restore

00:34.486 --> 00:37.263
which we'll talk about later on in this section.

00:37.263 --> 00:40.170
Okay, so if we use git checkout,

00:40.170 --> 00:42.300
but rather than checking out a branch,

00:42.300 --> 00:45.159
we instead checkout a particular commit hash

00:45.159 --> 00:46.497
like I've done here.

00:46.497 --> 00:49.494
Remember, we can view these commit hashes using git log.

00:49.494 --> 00:50.499
If we run this command,

00:50.499 --> 00:53.190
git checkout and then a commit hash,

00:53.190 --> 00:55.771
something quite peculiar happens.

00:55.771 --> 00:57.251
We're going to see this message

00:57.251 --> 01:01.020
that sounds very bad, detached HEAD.

01:01.020 --> 01:03.011
You are in detached HEAD state.

01:03.011 --> 01:04.410
You can look around,

01:04.410 --> 01:05.629
make experimental changes and commit them

01:05.629 --> 01:06.754
and you can discard any commits you make

01:06.754 --> 01:09.780
in the state without impacting any branches

01:09.780 --> 01:11.263
by switching back to a branch.

01:11.263 --> 01:13.247
Okay, so first of all,

01:13.247 --> 01:15.139
that sounds horrible, detached HEAD.

01:15.139 --> 01:16.494
It's not a bad thing.

01:16.494 --> 01:18.510
We'll talk about what exactly it means.

01:18.510 --> 01:20.242
But first, I wanna demonstrate this.

01:20.242 --> 01:23.947
So I'm going back to our first little repo,

01:23.947 --> 01:27.016
"The Great Gatsby" novel that I'm writing,

01:27.016 --> 01:28.492
my first novel.

01:28.492 --> 01:31.007
If you recall, we just have a single branch.

01:31.007 --> 01:33.393
I'm on master, so that's all that we have.

01:33.393 --> 01:34.498
And we have a bunch of commits.

01:34.498 --> 01:36.333
Let me do one line here.

01:38.250 --> 01:39.760
So we started with an outline

01:39.760 --> 01:41.498
and then I did chapter one

01:41.498 --> 01:43.487
and then I split chapter one into two,

01:43.487 --> 01:44.903
chapter one and chapter two.

01:44.903 --> 01:46.011
Then I renamed a character,

01:46.011 --> 01:48.004
character of Gatsby to Catsby,

01:48.004 --> 01:49.380
and then I added a mood board

01:49.380 --> 01:51.264
and I did a bunch of other stuff.

01:51.264 --> 01:55.635
Well, let's say, I wanna go back in time to when,

01:55.635 --> 01:57.645
I don't know, what would a good place be?

01:57.645 --> 02:02.010
Maybe when I renamed Gatsby to Catsby.

02:02.010 --> 02:04.486
So I want to undo that or at least I wanna see

02:04.486 --> 02:06.647
what things looked like back then.

02:06.647 --> 02:09.180
Right now, in my files,

02:09.180 --> 02:12.270
there is no Gatsby, it's Catsby here.

02:12.270 --> 02:15.330
If I look at the character's file,

02:15.330 --> 02:16.163
where are you?

02:16.163 --> 02:16.996
Here we are.

02:16.996 --> 02:18.983
Jay Catsby, chapter one.

02:18.983 --> 02:20.134
If I did a search,

02:20.134 --> 02:23.261
we would find Catsby in here and not Gatsby.

02:23.261 --> 02:24.499
There we are.

02:24.499 --> 02:25.973
All right, so if I wanna undo that,

02:25.973 --> 02:27.910
I wanna go back in time.

02:27.910 --> 02:30.007
I can take this commit hash.

02:30.007 --> 02:32.484
This is actually the abbreviated version.

02:32.484 --> 02:34.510
It's the first seven digits

02:34.510 --> 02:36.240
for each one of these commits

02:36.240 --> 02:38.310
and that's really all that Git needs.

02:38.310 --> 02:39.618
But I could also provide the entire thing

02:39.618 --> 02:41.475
if I just did git log

02:41.475 --> 02:44.190
and didn't do the abbreviated versions.

02:44.190 --> 02:45.023
So I could scroll down

02:45.023 --> 02:47.746
and find this commit hash here

02:47.746 --> 02:49.633
or just provide the first seven,

02:49.633 --> 02:52.875
one, two, three, four, five, six, seven.

02:52.875 --> 02:55.530
Okay, so I'm gonna copy that.

02:55.530 --> 02:56.745
And now, just gonna make sure

02:56.745 --> 03:00.030
I don't have any changes on here, okay, good.

03:00.030 --> 03:03.780
I'm gonna go check out that commit hash,

03:03.780 --> 03:06.494
git checkout, this old commit,

03:06.494 --> 03:08.247
and this is where I see that message.

03:08.247 --> 03:10.742
You are in detached HEAD state.

03:10.742 --> 03:11.887
You can look around, blah blah blah.

03:11.887 --> 03:13.410
I already read that.

03:13.410 --> 03:15.491
So let me show you a couple of things.

03:15.491 --> 03:17.913
First of all, if I type git log,

03:19.460 --> 03:20.943
let me do one line.

03:23.492 --> 03:24.325
We only have what?

03:24.325 --> 03:27.000
Five commits that are showing up right now

03:27.000 --> 03:28.394
because I have jumped back in time.

03:28.394 --> 03:31.003
I've traveled back to this commit.

03:31.003 --> 03:32.765
I don't know how many commits ago it was,

03:32.765 --> 03:35.015
six, seven, eight commits ago

03:35.015 --> 03:37.387
and there's only five commits at that point.

03:37.387 --> 03:39.260
This is where we are.

03:39.260 --> 03:41.754
Also, if I take a look at the files,

03:41.754 --> 03:44.395
well, I'm an idiot because I went back

03:44.395 --> 03:47.011
to the commit where we renamed to Catsby.

03:47.011 --> 03:48.002
I wanted to go back to the one

03:48.002 --> 03:51.246
before we were still Gatsby.

03:51.246 --> 03:54.628
So we see, Catsby is still everywhere.

03:54.628 --> 03:58.230
Okay, well, let's pick the commit before.

03:58.230 --> 04:00.246
So before we rename Gatsby to Catsby,

04:00.246 --> 04:03.988
I can go to this commit 4f1b

04:03.988 --> 04:04.821
and do the same thing.

04:04.821 --> 04:07.500
Git checkout that commit.

04:07.500 --> 04:09.255
And I'm still in this detached HEAD state.

04:09.255 --> 04:12.738
If I type git status, you can see HEAD detached.

04:12.738 --> 04:16.500
And if I take a look now, Gatsby,

04:16.500 --> 04:18.619
Gatsby is all that we see.

04:18.619 --> 04:20.008
Do I have another example?

04:20.008 --> 04:23.130
Here's Gatsby, we don't have Catsby at all.

04:23.130 --> 04:24.988
If I type git log,

04:24.988 --> 04:28.800
now, we only have four commits, okay?

04:28.800 --> 04:31.020
So I've jumped back in time.

04:31.020 --> 04:33.233
But don't fear, I haven't lost all of the other work

04:33.233 --> 04:34.965
that I had on the master branch.

04:34.965 --> 04:37.530
So what is this detached HEAD?

04:37.530 --> 04:38.748
Well, as we've learned earlier,

04:38.748 --> 04:40.387
but I'm just gonna review.

04:40.387 --> 04:44.151
Normally, HEAD points to a particular branch reference.

04:44.151 --> 04:45.148
Are you on master?

04:45.148 --> 04:47.734
Are you on bugfix or new feature?

04:47.734 --> 04:49.261
Whatever branch you're on,

04:49.261 --> 04:51.760
HEAD points to the branch reference,

04:51.760 --> 04:53.749
and then the branch reference itself

04:53.749 --> 04:55.129
points to the tip of a branch,

04:55.129 --> 04:57.990
the most recent commit on a branch.

04:57.990 --> 04:59.485
So if I'm on the master branch,

04:59.485 --> 05:01.440
HEAD points to master,

05:01.440 --> 05:02.994
and master points to some commit.

05:02.994 --> 05:04.259
If I make a new commit,

05:04.259 --> 05:07.372
master updates to point to the new commit,

05:07.372 --> 05:09.888
but HEAD still points at master, right?

05:09.888 --> 05:12.522
HEAD is still just pointing at that branch.

05:12.522 --> 05:15.503
If I switch branches, I go to bugfix.

05:15.503 --> 05:16.336
HEAD switches to bug fix,

05:17.895 --> 05:20.745
but HEAD is still pointing to a branch.

05:20.745 --> 05:23.280
And that's really the main takeaway so far.

05:23.280 --> 05:26.505
HEAD usually refers to a particular branch reference.

05:26.505 --> 05:28.800
It does not refer to a commit.

05:28.800 --> 05:29.739
HEAD is not pointing at this commit

05:29.739 --> 05:31.710
or this commit or that commit,

05:31.710 --> 05:33.748
HEAD is referring to the branch that we're on

05:33.748 --> 05:36.420
and the branch reference points to a commit.

05:36.420 --> 05:38.243
So now, back to this detached HEAD thing.

05:38.243 --> 05:40.680
Look at all those detached HEADS.

05:40.680 --> 05:43.728
I manually detached those just for this slide.

05:43.728 --> 05:47.507
Anyway, now, when we check out an earlier commit,

05:47.507 --> 05:50.258
what we're doing is actually changing HEAD

05:50.258 --> 05:52.376
to refer to a commit.

05:52.376 --> 05:54.900
So in this case, I'm just going back one commit.

05:54.900 --> 05:57.507
HEAD points directly at that commit,

05:57.507 --> 05:59.910
not at a branch reference.

05:59.910 --> 06:01.390
So it's kind of a weird state.

06:01.390 --> 06:03.731
It is not normal, it's not bad.

06:03.731 --> 06:05.742
But we can't always do the same things

06:05.742 --> 06:09.410
that we could do if we were on a branch.

06:09.410 --> 06:10.368
So we're not on a branch.

06:10.368 --> 06:11.484
We're just floating around.

06:11.484 --> 06:13.997
We're a detached HEAD.

06:13.997 --> 06:15.730
So I can just quickly show you

06:15.730 --> 06:17.460
what this looks like.

06:17.460 --> 06:19.490
If you recall, I actually showed that we can look

06:19.490 --> 06:22.761
at HEAD instead of this .git directory

06:22.761 --> 06:25.387
which is where all the git stuff is stored.

06:25.387 --> 06:27.976
We can look at head and earlier,

06:27.976 --> 06:30.498
when I first showed this a couple sections ago,

06:30.498 --> 06:34.267
we saw something like, I think refs/heads/master

06:34.267 --> 06:36.631
or oldies or whatever branch we were on.

06:36.631 --> 06:39.000
But right now, I'm in detached HEAD

06:39.000 --> 06:41.618
and HEAD is actually referring to a commit,

06:41.618 --> 06:43.253
the particular commit that I'm on,

06:43.253 --> 06:44.735
right, this commit.

06:44.735 --> 06:46.226
And that's abnormal.

06:46.226 --> 06:49.379
We're used to it pointing to an actual branch reference.

06:49.379 --> 06:52.004
Anyway, we are in detached HEAD at this point.

06:52.004 --> 06:52.887
In the next video,

06:52.887 --> 06:55.008
I'm gonna talk about what we can do here

06:55.008 --> 06:58.680
and why this could be useful, okay?

06:58.680 --> 06:59.964
So right now, we've gone back in time,

06:59.964 --> 07:03.519
but we're kind of just floating around some old commit.

07:03.519 --> 07:05.500
Next up, I'll talk about how we can branch off

07:05.500 --> 07:07.755
from here or undo things.

07:07.755 --> 07:09.033
So that's coming up.
